#edb/ref 17592186524808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524931 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cf2d06bc-9b8b-504e-b75b-69a639c7f6d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524935 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524935 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524935 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524935 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da0d6d19-9eb1-5179-970a-8af07c710dc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524963 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524963 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524963 #edb/ref 1071 "A company is selling a new product on three e-commerce platforms, platform $1,$ platform $2,$ and platform $3.$\n\n- $56%$ of customers bought the product on platform $1,$ and $96%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $14%$ of customers bought the product on platform $2,$ and $80%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $30%$ of customers bought the product on platform $3,$ and $90%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\nIf $45%$ of all customers are teenagers and $92%$ of teenage customers are satisfied with the product, what is the probability that a randomly chosen satisfied customer is a teenager? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524963 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "110563bd-a4e1-5dc9-8400-b537447d4a3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524963 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $T$ be the event that a customer is a teenager, $S$ be the event that a customer is satisfied, and ${P}_{1},{P}_{2},{P}_{3}$ be the event that a customer bought the product on platform $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({P}_{1})=0.56 &  & P(S|{P}_{1})=0.96 \\\\  & P({P}_{2})=0.14 &  & P(S|{P}_{2})=0.8 \\\\  & P({P}_{3})=0.3 &  & P(S|{P}_{3})=0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${P}_{1}⊔{P}_{2}⊔{P}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({P}_{1})+P({P}_{2})+P({P}_{3})=0.56+0.14+0.3=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of all customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $92%$ of teenage customers are satisfied, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|T)=0.92.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen satisfied customer is a teenager, which is represented as $P(T|S).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S|{P}_{1})P({P}_{1})+P(S|{P}_{2})P({P}_{2})+P(S|{P}_{3})P({P}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.92)(0.45)}{(0.96)(0.56)+(0.8)(0.14)+(0.9)(0.3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.414}{0.9196} \\\\  & ≈0.450,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524964 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524964 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524964 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524964 #edb/ref 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.1, & P({A}_{1})=0.15 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.2, & P({A}_{2})=0.46 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.6, & P({A}_{3})=0.39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524964 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "113bec1d-2c81-56b8-a44e-d46424e8b73b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524964 #edb/ref 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=0.15+0.46+0.39=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.2)(0.46)}{(0.1)(0.15)+(0.2)(0.46)+(0.6)(0.39)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.092}{0.341} \\\\  & ≈0.270,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524987 #edb/ref 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.25, & P({A}_{1})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=1, & P({A}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.34, & P({A}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57998d9d-2f79-5e0e-981a-e73726e34a06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524987 #edb/ref 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=0.3+0.3+0.4=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)(0.3)}{(0.25)(0.3)+(1)(0.3)+(0.34)(0.4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.3}{0.511} \\\\  & ≈0.587,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525053 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525053 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525053 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525053 #edb/ref 1071 "Apples produced by a particular farm are of three varieties: $1,2,$ and $3.$\n\n- $30%$ of the apples are of variety $1,$ and $45%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\n- $20%$ of the apples are of variety $2,$ and $15%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\n- $50%$ of the apples are of variety $3,$ and $10%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\nIf $75%$ of apples from the farm are sold to a local supermarket, and $20%$ of apples sold to the supermarket weigh more than $200\\,g,$ what is the probability that a randomly chosen apple that weighs more than $200\\,g$ is sold to the supermarket? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525053 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e178509e-4821-5890-a867-11dacf4cab24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525053 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that an apple is sold to the supermarket, $W$ be the event that the apple weights more than $200\\,g,$ and ${V}_{1},{V}_{2},{V}_{2}$ be the events that an apple is of variety $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({V}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{V}_{1})=0.45 \\\\  & P({V}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(W|{V}_{2})=0.15 \\\\  & P({V}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(W|{V}_{3})=0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${V}_{1}⊔{V}_{2}⊔{V}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({V}_{1})+P({V}_{2})+P({V}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $75%$ of apples from the farm are sold to a local supermarket, we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=0.75.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of apples sold to the supermarket weigh more than $200\\,g,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W|S)=0.2.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen apple that weigh more than $200\\,g$ is sold to the supermarket, which is represented as $P(S|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S|W) & =\\frac{P(W|S)P(S)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|S)P(S)}{P(W|{V}_{1})P({V}_{1})+P(W|{V}_{2})P({V}_{2})+P(W|{V}_{3})P({V}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.2)(0.75)}{(0.3)(0.45)+(0.2)(0.15)+(0.5)(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.215} \\\\  & ≈0.698,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525062 #edb/ref 1071 "A volleyball school for children splits the players into three teams, team $1,$ team $2,$ and team $3.$\n\n- $54%$ of the children are in team $1,$ and $20%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\n- $32%$ of the children are in team $2,$ and $30%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\n- $14%$ of the children are in team $3,$ and $15%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\nIf $10%$ of the children can play in all positions, and $94%$ of children that can play in all positions have been playing for over a year, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child who has been playing for over a year can play in all positions? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7a1a1db-a551-5627-b716-a412f2910020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525062 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $B$ be the event that a child can play in all positions, $I$ be the event that a child has been playing for over a year, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a child is in team $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.54 &  & P(I|{T}_{1})=0.2 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.32 &  & P(I|{T}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.14 &  & P(I|{T}_{3})=0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.54+0.32+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of children can play in all positions, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $94%$ of children who play in all positions have been playing for over a year, we have\n\n$$\nP(I|B)=0.94.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child who has been playing for over a year can play in all positions, which is represented as $P(B|I).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(I|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(I|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.94)(0.1)}{(0.2)(0.54)+(0.3)(0.32)+(0.15)(0.14)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.094}{0.225} \\\\  & ≈0.418,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
